.SH NAME
ns_connchan \- Manage connection channels\&.
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBns_connchan debug\fR \fIchannel\fR ?\fIlevel\fR?
.sp
\fBns_connchan detach\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan close\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan exists\fR \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan list\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR?
.sp
\fBns_connchan callback\fR ?\fB-timeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-receivetimeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-sendtimeout \fItime\fR\fR? \fIchannel\fR \fIcommand\fR \fIwhen\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan connect\fR ?\fB-cafile \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-capath \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-cert \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-hostname \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-insecure\fR? ?\fB-timeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-tls\fR? ?--? \fIhost\fR \fIport\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan listen\fR ?\fB-driver \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? ?\fB-bind\fR? ?--? \fIaddress\fR \fIport\fR \fIscript\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan open\fR ?\fB-cafile \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-capath \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-cert \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-driver \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-headers \fIsetId\fR\fR? ?\fB-hostname \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-insecure\fR? ?\fB-method \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-timeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-unix_socket \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-version \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan read\fR ?\fB-websocket\fR? \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan status\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan wsencode\fR ?\fB-binary\fR? ?\fB-fin 0|1\fR? ?\fB-mask\fR? ?\fB-opcode continue|text|binary|close|ping|pong\fR? ?--? \fImessage\fR
.sp
\fBns_connchan write\fR \fIchannel\fR \fImessage\fR
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION The command \fBns_connchan\fR allows one to detach the
current channel from a connection thread and manage the connection
outside the initiating connection thread\&. It allows one to write or read
to the channel, to define callbacks and to list open connections and
to close the connection\&. The read and write operations on this
channel will use directly the driver infrastructure which was in use
during the detach command\&.
.PP
The command allows e\&.g\&. to read from and to write to all
network drivers (such as plain HTTP channels and from SSL/TLS
connections)\&. It can be used to implement e\&.g\&. WebSockets or
asynchronous deliveries (e\&.g\&. h264 streams) including secure
connections\&. Therefore, this command is more general than the
approaches based on \fBns_conn channel\fR using plain Tcl channels\&.
.PP
NaviServer maintains an internal table per server to keep track of
the detached connection channels and to offer introspection to the
state of the detached channels\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBns_connchan debug\fR \fIchannel\fR ?\fIlevel\fR?
Sets or queries the debugging level of the current connection\&. When
set to "1", debug messages are written to the system log file;
setting it to "2" causes a separate debug file to be
created\&. Currently, this command primarily affects send operations,
although its functionality may be expanded in future releases\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan detach\fR
The command \fBns_connchan detach\fR unplugs the connection channel from
the current connection thread and stores it with a fresh handle name
in a per-virtual-server private table\&. The command returns the
created handle as result\&.
.sp
After this command was issued in a connection thread, all attempts to
access the connection socket directly (e\&.g\&. via \fBns_write\fR) will fail\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan close\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fIchannel\fR
Closes the named connection channel\&. The \fB-server\fR can be
used for cleanup of stale handles\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan exists\fR \fIchannel\fR
Returns 1 if the named connection channel exists, 0 otherwise\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan list\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR?
Returns a list of the currently detached connection channels for the
current or named \fIserver\fR\&.
.sp
Every list entry contains
.RS
.IP \(bu
name of the channel
.IP \(bu
name of the thread
.IP \(bu
start time of the initiating request,
.IP \(bu
driver,
.IP \(bu
the IP address of the client,
.IP \(bu
sent bytes,
.IP \(bu
received bytes,
.IP \(bu
the client data as provided via [ns_conn
clientdata],
.IP \(bu
the cmd name of the callback, or "" when no callback is registered,
.IP \(bu
the callback condition flags, or "" when no callback is registered\&.
.RE
.IP
When NaviServer is running in reverse proxy mode, the client IP
address is taken from the value as provided by the reverse proxy
server\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan callback\fR ?\fB-timeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-receivetimeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-sendtimeout \fItime\fR\fR? \fIchannel\fR \fIcommand\fR \fIwhen\fR
Registers a Tcl callback for the named connection \fIchannel\fR\&.
\fB-timeout\fR is the poll timeout (the maximum time between
two events), \fB-receivetimeout\fR is a
timeout for incoming packets, \fB-sendtimeout\fR is the timeout for
outgoing packets\&.  When \fB-sendtimeout\fR has the value of 0, a
read operation might return the empty string\&. A value larger than 0
might block the event processing for the specified time\&.
All timeouts values are specified in the form \fIsecs?:microseconds?\fR, or
\fIsecs\&.fraction\fR or as a number with a time unit\&.
.sp
The argument \fIwhen\fR consist of one or more characters
of r, w, e, or x, specifying, when the callback should fire\&.
.sp
When the callback is fired, the specified Tcl \fIcommands\fR will be
called with an additional argument, which is an indicator for the
reason of the call \fIwhen\fR\&. The value of \fIwhen\fR will be as
follows:
.RS
.IP \(bu
r - the socket is readable
.IP \(bu
w - the socket is writable
.IP \(bu
e - the socket has an exceptional condition
.IP \(bu
x - the server is shutting down
.IP \(bu
t - timeout received
.RE
.sp
When the callback exits, its return value determines, whether
the callback should be canceled or not\&. The return value is
interpreted as follows:
.RS
.IP \(bu
0 - the callback is canceled, and the channel is deleted
automatically (typically, when an error occurs)
.IP \(bu
1 - the callback will be used as well for further events
.IP \(bu
2 - the callback will be suspended\&. No further events will
be fired, but the channel is not deleted\&.
.RE
.TP
\fBns_connchan connect\fR ?\fB-cafile \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-capath \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-cert \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-hostname \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-insecure\fR? ?\fB-timeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-tls\fR? ?--? \fIhost\fR \fIport\fR
Opens a client connection channel to the specified \fIhost\fR
and \fIport\fR\&.  The command is similar to \fBns_connchan open\fR,
but does less: While
\fBns_connchan open\fR sends an HTTP request to the server denoted by
a URL, \fBns_connchan connect\fR just connects to the server
without sending any request data\&.
.sp
The option \fB-tls\fR will connection via TLS, otherwise
the connection is via plain socket\&. \fB-timeout\fR specifies the
timeout for establishing the connection (default 1 second)\&.
The timeout value \fItime\fR can be specified in the form
\fIsecs?:microseconds?\fR, or \fIsecs\&.fraction\fR,
or as a number with a time unit\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan listen\fR ?\fB-driver \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? ?\fB-bind\fR? ?--? \fIaddress\fR \fIport\fR \fIscript\fR
Opens a listening socket\&. Call the \fIscript\fR callback on incoming
connections\&. On success, this command returns a dict containing
\fIchannel\fR, \fIport\fR, \fIsock\fR and \fIaddress\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan open\fR ?\fB-cafile \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-capath \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-cert \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-driver \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-headers \fIsetId\fR\fR? ?\fB-hostname \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-insecure\fR? ?\fB-method \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-timeout \fItime\fR\fR? ?\fB-unix_socket \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?\fB-version \fIvalue\fR\fR? ?--? \fIurl\fR
.sp
Opens a connection channel to the specified \fIurl\fR\&.  The URL can
either be an HTTP or an HTTPS URL\&.
.sp
Note that the network \fIdriver\fR for the used protocol
specified in the \fIurl\fR must be loaded\&. For details, see
\fBCONFIGURATION\fR\&.
.sp
The \fIoptions\fR can be any of:
.sp
.RS
.TP
\fB-headers\fR \fIsetId\fR
A \fB\fIns_set\fR\fR of request header
fields\&.
.TP
\fB-hostname\fR \fIvalue\fR
Required for connecting to a
server with virtual hosting that provides multiple certificates via
SNI (Server Name Indication)\&.
.TP
\fB-method\fR \fImethod\fR
Specifies the HTTP method, by default
\fBGET\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-timeout\fR \fItime\fR
Specifies the timeout for
establishing the connection, by default 1 second\&. The timeout value
\fItime\fR is specified in the form \fIsecs?:microseconds?\fR,
or \fIsecs\&.fraction\fR, or as a number with a time unit\&.
.TP
\fB-unix_socket\fR \fIvalue\fR
a unix socket to connect to\&.
.TP
\fB-version\fR \fIvalue\fR
specifies the HTTP version (default 1\&.0)\&.
.RE
.TP
\fBns_connchan read\fR ?\fB-websocket\fR? \fIchannel\fR
Reads from the specified connection channel and returns the received data\&.
.sp
When the option \fB-websocket\fR is used, then the command
expects WebSocket frames\&. In this mode, the result of the command is a
Tcl \fBdict\fR containing the following elements:
.sp
\fIfin\fR status bit,
\fIframe\fR state (\fIincomplete\fR or \fIcomplete\fR),
\fIunprocessed\fR (received number of bytes in buffer not handled so far),
\fIfragments\fR (number of bytes in the WebSocket fragments buffer),
\fIhaveData\fR (boolean value to express that unprocessed
data might be sufficient for the next frame without an extra read
operation\&.
.sp
In case the frame is finished (\fIfin\fR status bit is set),
the dict contains as well the  WebSocket \fIopcode\fR and
the \fIpayload\fR of the frame\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan status\fR ?\fB-server \fIserver\fR\fR? \fIchannel\fR
Returns status information from the current or named \fIserver\fR\&.
The command returns a dict containing the following elements:
.sp
\fIdriver\fR (module name the driver),
\fIfragments\fR (for WebSocket read operations: number of bytes in the WebSocket fragments buffer),
\fIframebuffer\fR (for WebSocket read operations: number of bytes in the WebSocket frame buffer),
\fIpeer\fR (communication peer, the IP address of the other side of the channel),
\fIreceived\fR (number of bytes received),
\fIsendbuffer\fR number of bytes in the send buffer,
\fIsent\fR (number of bytes sent), and
\fIstart\fR (time when the connection started)\&.
When a callback is defined for this channel, the dict contains as well the
\fIcallback\fR and the
\fIcondition\fR on which the callback will be fired\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan wsencode\fR ?\fB-binary\fR? ?\fB-fin 0|1\fR? ?\fB-mask\fR? ?\fB-opcode continue|text|binary|close|ping|pong\fR? ?--? \fImessage\fR
Returns a binary websocket frame based on the provided information
in the form of a Tcl byte array\&.
When \fB-mask\fR is specified, the data will be masked with
random information\&. For the opcode \fIbinary\fR, the input is treated
as binary as well\&. When \fB-binary\fR is used, the data
will be treated as binary\&. This is e\&.g\&. necessary on
multi-segment messages, where later segments have the opcode
\fIcontinue\fR\&.
.TP
\fBns_connchan write\fR \fIchannel\fR \fImessage\fR
Writes the specified message to the given connection channel\&. The
command returns the number of bytes actually sent, which may be less
than the length of the provided message\&.
.sp
In cases of partial write operations, any unsent data is
automatically queued in the connection's send buffer\&. This queued
data is merged with any new data during the subsequent write
operations\&. To check the current status of the send buffer, use the
\fBns_conn status\fR command\&. If you need to drain the buffer
completely, repeatedly call \fBns_conn write\fR with an empty
message until the buffer is empty\&.
.PP
.SH "COMMON OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB-cafile\fR \fIvalue\fR
specifies for HTTPS requests a PEM file containing certificates to
validate the peer server (unless the option \fB-insecure\fR is used)\&. All
certificates in this file are trusted\&.  Typically, the file
has the name \fIca-bundle\&.crt\fR and contains the top-level certificates\&.
When the specified filename is not on an absolute location, the file is
assumed to be in the home directory of NaviServer\&. The default value
is \fIca-bundle\&.crt\fR, but can be altered in the \fBCONFIGURATION\fR file\&.
For more details, see
\fISSL_CTX_load_verify_locations\fR [https://docs\&.openssl\&.org/master/man3/SSL_CTX_load_verify_locations/]
from the OpenSSL documentation\&.
.TP
\fB-capath\fR \fIvalue\fR
specifies for HTTPS requests a directory containing trusted certificates
to validate the peer server (unless the option
\fB-insecure\fR is used)\&.
Each file in this directory must be in PEM format and must contain
exactly one certificate\&. When the specified directory is not an
absolute path, it is assumed to be in the home directory of NaviServer\&.
The default value is \fIcertificates\fR, but can be altered in the \fBCONFIGURATION\fR file\&.
For more details, see
\fISSL_CTX_load_verify_locations\fR [https://docs\&.openssl\&.org/master/man3/SSL_CTX_load_verify_locations/]
from the OpenSSL documentation\&.
.TP
\fB-cert\fR \fIvalue\fR
used for HTTPS URIs to use the specified client certificate\&. The
certificates must be in PEM format and must be sorted starting with
the subject's certificate (actual client or server certificate),
followed by intermediate CA certificates if applicable, and ending at
the highest level (root) CA\&.
.TP
\fB-hostname\fR \fIvalue\fR
used for HTTPS URIs to specify the hostname for the server
certificate\&. This option has to be used, when the host supports
virtual hosting, is configured with multiple certificates and supports
the SNI (Server Name Indication) extension of TLS\&.
.TP
\fB-insecure\fR
used for HTTPS URIs to specify that the server certificate should NOT be
verified\&. By default, the identity of the peer server is checked
for all HTTPS requests\&. If the verification process fails, the TLS/SSL handshake is
terminated with an alert message containing the reason for
the verification failure\&.
The default for this parameter can be specified in the \fBCONFIGURATION\fR file\&.
.PP
.SH CONFIGURATION
The \fBns_connchan\fR command uses the network driver configuration of
NaviServer\&. Therefore, the used network driver for the protocol must by
loaded via the NaviServer configuration file\&. When e\&.g\&. an outgoing
HTTPS request should be made, but the site administrator does not want
to listen on an HTTPS port, specify in the configuration of HTTPS the
\fIport\fR with value \fB0\fR\&.
.PP
The default values for the peer server validation parameters
\fB-cafile\fR,
\fB-capath\fR, and
\fB-insecure\fR can be configured in the configuration file as
documented in \fBns_http\fR\&. The configuration of the client
certificate management is as well shared with \fBns_http\fR\&.
For configuration details, see the section
\fIHTTP client security configuration\fR [\&.\&./\&.\&./manual/files//admin-config\&.html#subsection13]
